{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Emmanuel Esposito, Saeed Masoudian, Hao Qiu, Dirk van der Hoeven, Nicolò Cesa-Bianchi, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Intermediate observations are a close analogue of public price history that may predict delayed fills; useful for formalizing when predictable tails cease to be harmful.","title":"Delayed Bandits: When Do Intermediate Observations Help?","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/esposito23a.html","venue_year":"ICML/PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Ciara Pike-Burke, Shipra Agrawal, Csaba Szepesvari, Steffen Grunewalder","relevance":"Now verified from PMLR; relevant background for delayed/aggregated feedback and for distinguishing identifiable full-information fills from harder anonymous feedback.","title":"Bandits with Delayed, Aggregated Anonymous Feedback","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/pike-burke18a.html","venue_year":"ICML/PMLR 2018"},{"authors":"Marcelo J. Weinberger, Erik Ordentlich","relevance":"Older full-information delayed prediction reference already listed as unchecked; DBLP/DOI metadata verify bibliographic details and support the delayed-experts background section.","title":"On delayed prediction of individual sequences","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.2002.1013136","venue_year":"IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 2002"},{"authors":"Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy","relevance":"Very recent reduction-style delayed OCO paper with total-delay and outstanding-observation terms; may inform adaptive truncation/oracle inequalities beyond fixed H.","title":"A Reduction from Delayed to Immediate Feedback for Online Convex Optimization with Improved Guarantees","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02634","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Jiahao Zhang, Tao Lin, Weiqiang Zheng, Zhe Feng, Yifeng Teng, Xiaotie Deng","relevance":"Threshold learning with censored feedback and one-sided Lipschitz assumptions; potentially useful for the continuous quote geometry and limits of discretization-free methods.","title":"Learning Thresholds with Latent Values and Censored Feedback","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=qaKRfobbTg","venue_year":"ICLR 2024"},{"authors":"Jianyu Xu, Yining Wang, Xi Chen, Yu-Xiang Wang","relevance":"Recent dynamic pricing paper with adversarial censoring; adjacent terminology and techniques for censored observations, though not a direct delayed-fill characterization.","title":"Dynamic Pricing with Adversarially-Censored Demands","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06168","venue_year":"arXiv 2025, revised 2026"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The comparator over the whole prefix is a single fixed quote, so cross-block rewards and previous pending quotes can invalidate a naive per-block regret decomposition. Horizon-aware learner families may require an explicit diagonalization argument.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower bound formalization","randomized learner conditioning"],"summary":"Turn CL-016 into a precise theorem. Fix a universal process-unaware learner, choose dominant deterministic blocks 0^{N_i} c_i with c_i in {1/2,1} and M_i=o(N_i), condition on the fixed prefix and the first N_i zeros, use the finite two-continuation lemma to choose c_i causing at least N_i/4 current-block regret, then prove earlier blocks and pending rewards cost only O(M_i). State separately for anytime learners and horizon-indexed learner families.","title":"Prove the concatenated universal block lower bound","why_promising":"This is the closest current route to a genuine necessity-style statement: it converts the accepted finite indistinguishability gadget into a class-uniform impossibility without reviving the false instance-wise claim."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The direction may collapse too: known-law anytime learners might be able to schedule near-optimal quotes by doubling or by Blackwell-style calibration, making no tail condition necessary even without horizon knowledge. Conversely, examples may depend on artificial noncomputability or rapidly changing horizon optima.","suggested_lenses":["quantifier audit","counterexample search","online-to-batch reduction"],"summary":"Investigate the regime between oracle collapse and universal learning: the learner knows the exogenous process law but not the horizon, and must use one anytime policy with pseudo-regret o(T) for every prefix. Either prove a generic anytime collapse via grids/doubling over horizon-optimal fixed quotes, or construct a known process whose horizon-optimal quote switches across dominant horizons so that no single quote sequence tracks all prefixes.","title":"Horizon-oblivious process-aware learnability","why_promising":"The notes identify horizon awareness as the fatal reason instance-wise necessity is vacuous. Removing only that assumption is a clean, separable quantifier change that may yield the weakest nontrivial necessity theorem."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A proposed modulus can become tautological if it merely restates minimax regret. It must produce either an upper-bound algorithm or a lower-bound lemma with checkable hypotheses on histories, continuation values, and payoff scales.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","Le Cam/Yao lower bounds","upper-lower matching"],"summary":"Define a decision-relevant delayed-tail or value-of-information quantity for a process class: after a history, measure pairs of continuations that remain observationally identical until after H but have separated optimal fixed quotes or separated comparator values. Prove a two-continuation lower bound in terms of this separation and compare it with sufficient quantities Γ_T(H), Δ_T(H), and skipped payoff mass.","title":"Replace raw tails by an information obstruction","why_promising":"All accepted counterexamples show that long tails alone are harmless when low-payoff, sparse, or predictable. A continuation-separation modulus directly targets the missing ingredient: unpredictability that affects the optimal quote."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Pathwise regret is a different problem, so it cannot be presented as a fix to the original pseudo-regret theorem without explicit restatement. Sparse vanishing-payoff examples may still refute unweighted β-necessity, requiring a weighted or value-based tail instead.","suggested_lenses":["regret notion comparison","stochastic mixture lower bound","counterexample transfer"],"summary":"Analyze the alternative regret E[sup_a sum_t r_t(a) - sum_t r_t(a_t)]. Revisit the random two-continuation block under this pathwise comparator; unlike pseudo-regret, the hidden terminal state should remain hard. Then test whether β, γ, or an information-weighted tail becomes necessary under this stronger hindsight comparator.","title":"Pathwise comparator variant","why_promising":"The oracle collapse and stochastic-mixture warning are specific to sup_a E[reward]. If the original motivation wanted hindsight over realized prices, this variant may restore a meaningful tail characterization."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Combining many truncation horizons can introduce extra logarithmic factors and feedback alignment problems; rewards for different H correspond to different surrogate games. Existing bandit/OCO skipping results may not transfer directly despite public prices.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","oracle inequality","literature mapping"],"summary":"Build an adaptive finite-grid algorithm that does not require a known H or known β decay. Target an oracle bound like inf_H {sqrt(T(H+1) log K)+T/K+Γ_T(H)} plus polylog factors, using multi-scale truncation, sleeping experts over H, or skip-set reductions. Map Joulani et al. 2013/2016, Gyorgy-Joulani 2021, Flaspohler et al. 2021, and Ryabchenko-Attias-Roy 2026 to the public-price full-information setting.","title":"Adaptive truncation and skipping oracle bounds","why_promising":"This would strengthen the accepted sufficiency theorem from a hand-tuned H(T) proof to a practical anytime theorem and may identify the right algorithmic tail penalty."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The expected contribution must stay small for all horizons while the essential supremum remains large on positive-probability histories. It is easy to accidentally create linear comparator value or a process-aware optimal quote that does not actually achieve small regret.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","horizon calculations","essential-sup stress test"],"summary":"Finish CE-006 by constructing a rare-branch process: with tiny probability p_n reveal a code leading to a long positive-payoff delay L_n, with p_n L_n summable or sublinear over horizons, while conditional on the rare code the tail probability is one for arbitrarily large h. Compute β, γ, Δ_T, Γ_T, and pseudo-regret for a simple learner.","title":"Rare-history essential-sup counterexample","why_promising":"It would separate uniform conditional tail assumptions from ex-ante learnability even at positive payoff scale, clarifying whether γ fails because of predictability, rarity, or both."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The reward functions a 1{threshold>a} have discontinuities, so one-sided Lipschitz tools may require distributional smoothness absent from the adversarial/public-price model. The standard T/K downward grid bound may already be minimax without extra assumptions.","suggested_lenses":["threshold geometry","discretization lower bounds","smoothness assumptions"],"summary":"Explore whether threshold/censored-feedback geometry can improve or qualify the grid discretization step. Study f_t^H(a)=a 1{M_t^H>a}, where M_t^H=max_{s≤H} P_{t+s}, and ask whether adaptive partitions, one-sided Lipschitz assumptions, or censored-threshold results yield better continuum regret than T/K, or prove an adversarial lower bound showing the grid loss is unavoidable.","title":"Continuous quote geometry beyond uniform grids","why_promising":"This is independent of tail necessity and could sharpen the sufficiency theorem. The newly checked threshold/censored papers give terminology and possible assumptions for a non-adversarial refinement."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
